{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213005",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x⊆y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our ordered pairs in turn.\n\n- The ordered pair $(\\{2,1\\},\\{2,1\\})$ is an element of $R$ since $\\{2,1\\}⊆\\{2,1\\}.\\,✓$\n\n- The ordered pair $(\\{2,1\\},\\{2,4\\})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since $\\{2,1\\}⊈\\{2,4\\}.\\,\\times $\n\n- The ordered pair $(\\{2,1\\},\\{2,8\\})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since $\\{2,1\\}⊈\\{2,8\\}.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
